## The three loops — how this workspace orients, learns, and stays honest

This kit doesn't just run forward (you → agents → code); it has **back-flow** — three loops
that make the workspace get better the longer you use it. Each is a cheap **sensor** (a hook
that *notices*) paired with an **actuator** (a skill that *acts*, proposing every
version-controlled edit before making it — nothing is written silently). See
[`docs/adr/0002-the-three-loops-auto-growing-workspace.md`](docs/adr/0002-the-three-loops-auto-growing-workspace.md).

| Loop | Sensor (notices) | Actuator (acts) | What it gives you |
|---|---|---|---|
| **1 · Onboarding** | `onboard.sh` (SessionStart) | `/orient`, `/install` | install → *alive*: a real `CONTEXT.md` from the code, per folder shape |
| **2 · Learning** | `suggest-memory.sh` (Stop) | `/remember` | a lesson learned once is never re-explained |
| **3 · Freshness** | `check-freshness.sh` (SessionStart) | `drift-audit` | docs never silently drift from code |

- **`/orient`** reads an existing repo and generates a trustworthy `CONTEXT.md` (data model,
  surface, glossary). The single highest-leverage thing you can do on a new repo.
- **`/remember`** routes a durable lesson to its home — a **rule** here in `CLAUDE.md`, an
  **orientation fact** to `CONTEXT.md`, a **decision** to an ADR, a **preference** to
  `.claude/memory/`. `.claude/memory/MEMORY.md` is the session-loaded index; team memories
  are committed, personal ones (`*.local.md`) are gitignored.
- **`drift-audit`** is the honest mirror when the freshness sensor flags a stale doc.
- **`/status`** is the cockpit: a one-screen read-only view of all of the above — what's
  wired, what the crew has learned, where docs drifted, and the one command that closes each
  gap.

The payoff compounds: every lesson captured is a mistake never repeated and context never
re-paid for. Treat a correction you'll hit again as a `/remember` candidate, not a one-off.

